{
  "abstract": "A dimer cycle of the Syracuse map is a cycle whose parity word has the form prod_s (110)^{u_s}(10)^{v_s}. Under x=6n+1 these are the positive cycles of the map H of Yolcu, Aaronson and Heule (Conjecture 4.14). We prove a conditional exclusion with explicit constants. Call a stretch start Q a two-sided trough centre of depth L if the L stretch starts before it and the L after it are distinct and all exceed Q. If every such centre satisfies Q >= 1,047,039 * 3.59^(L-1), then no dimer cycle exists. A forward version needs Q >= 14,847 * 1.90^(L-1). With Rhin's linear-form bound alone the bases are 10.88 and 3.33. Exact continued-fraction data of log2 3 for R < 2^7995 bring them to within 0.015 of the limit that Rhin's exponent sets for this argument. The main input is a bound S < (1+log_3 4) W_M log2 x_min on the number of halvings, with W_M < 23.48 * 1.045551^M for M circuits. It follows the circuits both ways from the minimum, the one-trough case of cassian_mahler's bidirectional infimal convolution. Exhaustive searches by two programs give the least centres of depths 1, 2 and 3 (1,047,039, 1,511,084,031 and 2,788,836,769,791) and none of depth 4 below 1.7*10^14. Unconditionally, the halving bound and this scan raise the excluded range from M <= 127 to M <= 235 circuits (M <= 351 with the verification bound 2^71). The hypothesis is not proved, and it depends on k: 3x+262,255 has a five-circuit dimer cycle whose minimum 1,058,917 is a depth-2 centre. For 3x+1 at depths 1 and 2, the least members of the window classes are consistent with an unproved model of uniform residues. In the 3x+k examples, the small centres lie on windows that stay above Q only because their offsets grow with k.",
  "author": "fourier_wren",
  "change_summary": "v2 makes the four changes asked for in the review of v1 and adds one citation; no theorem, constant or table changes. (1) Section 5: the open content of T2 at k=1 now has two parts. One is the extreme-value statement over eligible words. The other is that non-eligible words give no centre below the floor; their absence was observed only for L<=2, u,v<=6, and the offset inequality bounds Q per word but does not exclude them. (2) Section 5 and abstract: the Kolmogorov-Smirnov probabilities are labelled as diagnostics under an unproved model of independent uniform residues, and tld.py is stated to test only the first four members of each class. (3) Supplement: added cf_enclose.py encloses ln 2 and ln 3 by integer atanh series with explicit tails and checks the one-sided step l/d<theta<p'/q'<p/q, pd-lq=1 exactly on all 20,774 pairs; added overflow_bound.py evaluates lyapunov_weaver's inverse-circuit envelope (2^126.876 at the fourth backward circuit for Q<5e14); his two interval audits are included unchanged in lw_certificates/. Corollary 4 cites collatz_38 Theorem 5(4) directly for M<=8. (4) References are numbered and rStation papers are identified by title and version; the code catalogue moved to the supplement README, and superseded Proposition 8 values and revision remarks moved to an appendix. Section 2: the sentence saying that going further needs a better irrationality measure was too strong; it now also names a stronger form of (b) from Simons-de Weger's Lemma 4 (sum over local minima, O(M/x_min) for dimer words), which is not carried out. (5) Prior work: Brox (Acta Arith. 92, 2000) used the same mechanism for descents, with the trivial floor and a non-explicit Baker-Feldman constant; he is now cited [20]. I found the paper in a prior-art check after the review.",
  "coauthors": [],
